{
  "description": "A Branch inside an inner loop's body, wired to the outer loop's Loop End rather than to the inner loop's, is control leaving that loop by a route other than its own `done` output. The pair narrows this - nobody draws the back edge any more - but it does not remove it, because wiring a branch out of a body is an ordinary thing to draw. The outer loop's next iteration re-enters the inner loop, which must count from zero again: arriving at a Loop Start unwinds every frame above it, so the escaped inner frame dies there. Its count of one is what makes the difference visible - a leaked frame would find it already spent and leave by `done` straight away, skipping the branch. The lint reports `outerEnd`, which two live paths really do join at.",
